; Tutorial.inix -- the first walk through FileDir and the Player.
;
; WHAT THIS FILE IS
;
; One step to a section, in the order a person would do them. Each step says
; three things: what a narrator should explain, which key to press, and what a
; screen reader says in answer.
;
;   Say=    the narration, in plain words
;   Key=    the key to press, written the way the documents write keys
;   Hear=   one line of what a screen reader says, repeated for each line it says
;   Note=   a remark for the reader of the written version; not spoken
;
; The Hear lines are the point of the format. They come from real speech
; histories rather than from anybody's idea of what ought to be said, so the
; written tutorial matches what a person actually hears, and the same file can
; be replayed later to check that it still does.
;
; The settings assumed are the factory ones, with tutor messages limited to
; controls that are not standard, and punctuation not announced.
;
; The two searches use a real directory: the Access On show, whose episodes
; mention the BrailleNote Evolve in a title and the Ray Ban Meta Glasses only in
; a summary. That contrast is the whole reason both searches exist.
;
; makeTutorial.py turns this into a section of Tutorials.md.
; sayTutorial.cmd turns it into a spoken walk, clearly announced as a
; simulation.

; The feed section is read from whichever script has one, and describes the
; whole set rather than this tutorial. Base is where the files will live when
; published; leave it as it is to keep the feed working from a folder on disk.
[feed]
Title=Homer Tools Walkthroughs
Description=Short simulated walks through FileDir and the Player, for people who work by ear. Each one is a few minutes, with something to try at the end.
Author=Jamal Mazrui
Email=empowermentzone@gmail.com
Base=

[about]
Title=0 - Overview
Intro=What FileDir is, in a paragraph; the two reader keys every walk assumes; then the table of contents, one line per walk.
Setup=Nothing is needed; this walk is listened to.
Homework=Listen to the walks in order; each one assumes the ones before it.

[step]

Say=FileDir is a file manager for working by ear. A folder is a list you arrow through; a file is a line you hear; tagging marks files for the next command; two searches find a file by its name or by everything known about it; and the Homer Player plays what a folder or a page holds. Every key is named for a word of its command, and every dialog works one way.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Two keys before anything else, both the reader's own. If a line goes by too fast, Insert plus Up Arrow says it again.
Key=Insert+UpArrow
Hear=(the last line, read a second time)
Say=Nothing changed; the key only repeats.

[step]

Say=And if you lose your place, Insert plus Tab says where you are: the control, its state, its position, and any hint it carries. The walks are heard with those hints off, the way most people work.
Key=Insert+Tab
Hear=(the current control, with its state and position)

[step]

Say=Now the table of contents. I say the number and the title; the reader says what the walk covers.
Key=
Hear=
[step]

Say=One, User Interface.
Key=
Hear=FileDir's window, the list you hear, the dialogs that all work one way, and where help is, each named and then shown by the reader; then the rules every key follows, so a key can be guessed before it is learned.
[step]

Say=Two, Install and Launch.
Key=
Hear=The download, the installer's pages and boxes, and FileDir opening by itself.
[step]

Say=Three, Open a Folder and Find a File.
Key=
Hear=Arrowing a folder, jumping to a file by name, and opening it.
[step]

Say=Four, Tag Files and Copy Them.
Key=
Hear=The semicolon tags; a command then acts on every tagged file at once.
[step]

Say=Five, Play a Page's Media in the Homer Player.
Key=
Hear=A podcast page's episodes found, queued and played; the two searches; extra info.
[step]

Say=Six, Rename Files to What Is Inside.
Key=
Hear=One want: files named by a camera or a scanner, called by what they are instead.
[step]

Say=Seven, Convert Between Formats.
Key=
Hear=One want: a Word document as a web page, or a PDF as plain text, without opening either.
[step]

Say=Eight, Ask About a File.
Key=
Hear=One want: what is in a long document, without reading it all.
[step]

Say=Nine, Conclusion.
Key=
Hear=What to carry away, the words FileDir uses, the help built in, and where to learn more.
[step]

Say=Ten walks, each under five minutes, under an hour together: the overview, the user interface, seven tasks, and the conclusion. They are a course, not a reference: each one assumes those before it.
Key=
Hear=
